# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: encoding-probe-artifacts-and-macos-forward -- encoding probe artefacts carry other jobs' files; macOS has no FORWARD step
#+STATUS: NEXT
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: infra
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-probe-script-defects
#+DATE: 2026-10-08

* Symptom (found verifying MR !467, jobs 17033123929-32)

1. The Windows runner reuses one build directory and the encoding probe
   scripts never clean dist/encoding/, so each job's artefact also holds
   older files of OTHER jobs: 17033123930's lispsys-bom-autocad-Windows.txt
   and autocad-Windows.txt date from 15:07-15:08 (other runs), 17033123932's
   e1f/* and autocad-Windows.txt were written by 17033123931, and
   clautolisp-Linux.txt dates from 2026-08-21. A file cited from an
   artefact must be checked by timestamp -- easy to cite the wrong run.
2. run-lispsys-bom-probe.ps1 writes its forward files under dist/e1/,
   which is not in the artefact: only the reported SIZE lines are evidence
   for that job, not the bytes.
3. scripts/run-encoding-experiment.sh (macOS) has no FORWARD step (only the
   .ps1 has one), so "BricsCAD macOS forwards nothing" is never measured end
   to end.
4. The -x way prints only "-x LISPSYS n", not FORWARDED-ARG / -CCS.

* Fix

Clean dist/encoding/ (and dist/e1/) at the start of every encoding probe
script, include dist/e1/ in the artefact paths, add the FORWARD step to the
.sh experiment, print FORWARDED-ARG / -CCS on the -x way too.

* Fix (2026-10-22)

1. Stale files: run-encoding-experiment.ps1 / .sh remove dist/encoding/ before
   anything else, run-lispsys-bom-probe.ps1 removes dist/encoding/ AND
   dist/e1/. Each encoding job runs exactly one of these scripts, so the
   artefact now holds this job's files only.
2. dist/e1/: encoding:experiment:lispsys-bom:autocad:windows (the one job that
   runs run-lispsys-bom-probe.ps1) has its own artifacts paths
   [dist/encoding/, dist/e1/]; the shared .encoding-artifacts template keeps
   dist/encoding/ only, so the other encoding jobs never ship a stale e1/.
3. macOS FORWARD: run-encoding-experiment.sh has the .ps1's FORWARD step for a
   CAD backend: lispsys-forward-probe.lsp under -Efile-write utf-8, three alfe
   runs ("FORWARD alfe-load" -l on an entry file holding (alfe-load "<probe>"),
   "FORWARD -l" -l <probe>, "FORWARD -x" -l <probe> with E1F_MODE=x plus the
   same -x form as the .ps1), E1_DIR = dist/encoding/e1f, the lines kept by the
   same filter (ENC / ENC-PROBE DONE / WARN / ERROR / BOOTSTRAP-FAILED /
   FAILED) and prefixed "[FORWARD ...] " like the .ps1. Checked locally with
   bash -n and by running the step against the clautolisp backend (--host
   nihil): the three runs start, E1_DIR / E1F_MODE / the -x form reach the
   probe (the -x way runs e1f-x only, the -l way runs e1f-run at load); the
   clautolisp backend has no alfe-load and no LISPSYS, which is why the step
   is skipped for it, as in the .ps1.
4. -x way: lispsys-forward-probe.lsp's e1f-x now prints the same header as
   e1f-run, "-x LISPSYS n FORWARDED-ARG ... FORWARDED-CCS ..." (one helper,
   e1f--header, for both).

Closes when the next encoding run shows artefacts holding only that job's
files (dist/e1/ included for lispsys-bom) and the macOS job reports the three
FORWARD ways.

Seen on the way, filed apart (deferred-probe-scripts-host-mock): the
clautolisp backend of these probe scripts passes --host mock, which alfe now
rejects (cador, cadtui, nihil), so a clautolisp run reports nothing.
